```c
static jv jvp_string_empty_new(uint32_t length) {
  jvp_string* s = jvp_string_alloc(length);
  s->length_hashed = 0;
  memset(s->data, 0, length);
  jv r = {JVP_FLAGS_STRING, 0, 0, 0, {&s->refcnt}};
  return r;
}
```

`jvp_string_alloc(length)` allocates `sizeof(jvp_string) + length + 1` bytes. The `+1` is for the null terminator. But `jvp_string_empty_new` does `memset(s->data, 0, length)` — it only zeroes `length` bytes, NOT `length+1`. So the null terminator byte (at `s->data[length]`) is NOT zeroed. 

